\description{
\code{sus_climate_aggregate()} aggregates meteorological data to DATASUS health data
using epidemiologically rigorous temporal strategies. The function links each
health record to the nearest climate station (by Euclidean distance) and applies
the requested temporal window.

This is the climate-health integration step. Run it after
\code{\link[=sus_spatial_join]{sus_spatial_join()}} (health side, stage \code{"spatial"}) and after
\code{\link[=sus_climate_inmet]{sus_climate_inmet()}} / \code{\link[=sus_climate_fill_inmet]{sus_climate_fill_inmet()}} /
\code{\link[=sus_climate_compute_indicators]{sus_climate_compute_indicators()}} (climate side, stage \code{"climate"}).
Hourly climate data are first aggregated to daily resolution. Windows are
retroactive (see Details). It is built for INMET-style columns; UNIPLU data
(\code{\link[=sus_climate_uniplu]{sus_climate_uniplu()}}) only provide \code{rainfall_mm}.
}
\details{
\subsection{Temporal Strategies Epidemiological Foundations}{

The choice of strategy should reflect the hypothesized biological mechanism:
\itemize{
\item \strong{exact}: Immediate effects (heat stroke, hemorrhagic stroke)
\item \strong{discrete_lag}: Known delayed effect (e.g., temperature 7 days before influences dengue)
\item \strong{moving_window}: Cumulative exposure without specific lag.
\item \strong{offset_window}: Defined incubation period (e.g., temperature 14-7 days before death)
\item \strong{distributed_lag}: Distributed lag analysis (DLNM); generates exposure matrix for \code{dlnm::crossbasis()}
\item \strong{degree_days}: Thermal threshold for vector development. GDD above temperature base accumulates over window_days.
\item \strong{seasonal}: Seasonal climate patterns for ecological or long-term studies
\item \strong{threshold_exceedance}: Counts days exceeding threshold (ideal for heatwaves)
\item \strong{cold_wave_exceedance}: Counts days below threshold (ideal for cold extremes in southern regions)
\item \strong{weighted_window}: Weighted mean modeling biological decay of exposure
}
}

\subsection{Regional Climate Considerations}{

The function automatically adapts to Brazil's diverse climates:

\strong{Tropical (North, Northeast):} Mean temperature 24-28degC, recommended temp_base 20degC (human health),
heatwave threshold Tmax > 35degC, high autocorrelation.

\strong{Subtropical (Center-West, Southeast):} Mean temperature 18-26degC, recommended temp_base 18degC,
heatwave threshold Tmax > 32degC, moderate autocorrelation.

\strong{Temperate (South):} Mean temperature 12-24degC, recommended temp_base 15degC,
heatwave threshold Tmax > 30degC, \strong{coldwave threshold Tmin < 5degC} (critical),
low autocorrelation.
}

\subsection{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item \code{cold_wave_exceedance} needs \code{window_days} internally, but the argument
validation currently only enforces/returns it for the four strategies
listed under \code{window_days}; check the result when using this strategy.
\item Variables are collapsed to one value per municipality and day using
variable-specific rules (sum for rainfall/radiation/degree-days, max for
Tmax and heat indices, min for Tmin and cold indices, circular mean for
wind direction, mean otherwise).
}
}

\subsection{Causal Inference & Look-Ahead Bias}{

This function uses \strong{retroactive windows} (t-W, t), never symmetric windows (t-W, t+W).
The climate of day t+7 cannot cause a health event on day t. This design prevents
look-ahead bias, a common methodological error in environmental epidemiology.
}
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
# Prepare data
df_health <- sus_data_import() |>
  sus_data_clean_encoding() |>
  sus_data_standardize() |>
  sus_spatial_join(level = "munic")

df_climate <- sus_climate_inmet(years = 2023, uf = "AM") |>
  sus_climate_fill_inmet(target_var = "all", parallel = TRUE)

# Example 1: Exact match (same-day temperature)
df_exact <- sus_climate_aggregate(
  health_data = df_health,
  climate_data = df_climate,
  climate_var = "tair_dry_bulb_c",
  temporal_strategy = "exact"
)

# Example 2: Moving window (cumulative exposure, RECOMMENDED)
df_moving <- sus_climate_aggregate(
  health_data = df_health,
  climate_data = df_climate,
  temporal_strategy = "moving_window",
  window_days = 14
)

# Example 3: Regional adaptation (auto-detect)
df_regional <- sus_climate_aggregate(
  health_data = df_health,
  climate_data = df_climate,
  climate_region = "auto",
  temporal_strategy = "threshold_exceedance",
  window_days = 7
)

# Example 4: Cold waves (southern regions)
df_coldwave <- sus_climate_aggregate(
  health_data = df_health_south,
  climate_data = df_climate_south,
  climate_region = "temperate",
  temporal_strategy = "cold_wave_exceedance",
  window_days = 7,
  threshold_value = 5
)
}

}
